#---
# purpose: "A map pattern's binary key reaches the process heap only where a clause reads it"
#---
#
# A map pattern keyed by a binary -- `%{"key" => value}` -- is decided against a
# copy of those bytes on the process heap. `describe/1` is called only with
# tuples, so its first clause answers every call and the map clause is never
# reached. The values come out of the mailbox, so each call is decided at run
# time against the whole head.
#
# A map pattern's key is the one binary constant a plan prepares; a binary a
# guard compares against is built where it is asked, on every evaluation.
#
# `bitstring_allocs` counts the copies the three calls made, and each path owns
# its own count:
#
# - `interp` decides the head through the dispatch plan and builds a prepared
#   key the first time a question reads it, so the key no call reaches is never
#   copied: `0`.
# - `run` and `build` lower the key as a constant at the head of the function,
#   which is one copy per call: `3`.
def describe({a, b}), do: a + b
def describe(%{"key" => value}), do: value
def describe(_), do: 0

def relay() do
  receive do
    value -> value
  after
    1000 -> 0
  end
end

def main() do
  send(self(), {1, 2})
  send(self(), {3, 4})
  send(self(), {5, 6})
  before = Process.heap_alloc_stats().bitstring_allocs
  assert(describe(relay()) == 3)
  assert(describe(relay()) == 7)
  assert(describe(relay()) == 11)
  count_after = Process.heap_alloc_stats().bitstring_allocs
  dbg(count_after - before)
end
